Note on configure and make.

A.  Configure.

 There are three configure-* scripts, configure-libs, configure-model and configure-scr.  In general, these scripts performs the following functions:

 1.  Generates Makefile files in each subdirectories from Makefile.in file.  

      Makefile specifies how to compile (compile options) and link 
      (specification of libraries) program.  These specifications are dependent
      on the machine the program is compiled and the architecture of the 
      machines, and are defined in opt_libs/options-machine_architchture files.

 2.  Create *.sh or shell script (without suffix) from *.sh.in or *.in files

      in some of the directories (con directory for libs, co2, mtn and others
      for srcs).  The *.sh is a shell script that executes 
      simple programs (to be explained later).  These scripts require absolute
      path and some machine dependent options, which are also taken from
      opt_libs/options-machine_architecture files.

Once configure is executed, it needs to be re-executed only when *.in or 
opt_libs/options files are modified.

B.  make.

 Makefile is generated in each subdirectory and performs the following 
 functions:

 1.  Compile programs and generates executable *.x or library *.a.

 2.  Occasionally executes scripts *.sh generated by configure.

 3.  Unpacks packed binary data files.

C.  make command options.

 The command 'make' and its options can be issued from any subdirectories.  
 It will perform the functions described above to all the directories below 
 the current directory.  'make' detects the most recently modified programs and 
 recompiles only those modified.  This makes the program change and model
 compilation very efficient.

 1. 'make clean' deletes the files generated by previous 'make'.  This is 
 useful when executables or libraries needs to be generated again after
 changing Makefile or configure options, and when you do not know which
 programs are affected by the changes. Do not 'make clean' when you are testing
 the change in programs, since make automatically detects the changed programs.
 'make clean' does not delete the files generated by configure.

 2. 'make dist_clean' deletes the files generated by configure and make.

 3. 'make depend' generates dependency and added to Makefile.  This is useful
 when the automatic detection for compilation needs to be extended to include
 files.  This options is normally not performed, since 'make clean' and 'make'
 procedure is much safer when include files are changed.

 4.  'make gsm_mpi_clean' and 'make rsm_mpi_clean' cleans only mpi related
 directories for gsm and rsm, respectively.

 5.  Examine Makefile in each directory and find other more unconventional
 options.

 6.  You can still reduce compile time even you are making many changes 
     to include files or to the define.h file.  You cd to src directory
     and then type:

      touch `grep -l ??? */*.F`

        where ??? is the define variable or include file name.
        What this command does is to change the date of the files
        that contain ??? key word, and faking that these files are
        modified, thus compiling only those files again.  Please do
        not forget '-l' since grep returns file name.  If it is missing
        it will generate many empty file and will not work.

D.  What actually takes place.

 1.  In libs, make will generates the following:

		1-1.  grib packing and unpacking library (w3lib.a)
    1-2.  model utility library (modelib.a)
    1-3.  ncar utility library (ncaru.a)
    1-4.  executables for various utilities (*.x in etc directory)
    1-5.  scripts to run the executables (files without suffix in etc directory)

     Within the utility scripts, the some essential for running the
     model and some are rarely used.  Model will run without essential utility
     scripts/executables.

     Very important (used by all the run scripts)
          date
          fhour
          incdte
          inchour
          scnvrt
          mpiset
          rmpiset
          force_grib_date_mon
          nt

     Important (used by some run scripts, frequently used utility) 
          grmean
          grvar
          grmap
          grib1.*  (tables used in grmean and grmap)
          grmap
          mkdef
          mkdep
          prmap
          wgrib
          jd
          dif

      Inrequencly used (not normlly used)
          cray2ie3
          define_update
          getfile
          grsplit
          grtran
          ibm2ie3
          pentads
          renam
          sigphys

 2.  in gsm/rsm directory

     Creates libraries and executables.  The libraries are generated first
     and executables are made last, since it links libraries.  In addition,
     co2 program (also mtn program for gsm and gdas) is executed.  co2 program
     interpolates co2 profile in vertical to the model levels.  mtn program
     computes orography for global model resolution.  

     cldtune program is not used.  This program is necessary to tune the
     cloud relative humidity relationships using forecast data.

     Occasionally, the compile fails due to various reasons.  On some machines,
     make does not fail and keep on going to the end.  This results in missing
     executables when the program is executed.  When this occurrs, you need
     to go to the directory that the compilation is failed and type 'make'. 
     You will see why the compilation is failing.  Fix and type 'make' to
     continue.

3.  in runs directory

    Create run scripts.  All the run scripts template (*.in) are located
    in expscr.  configure-scr script needs to be executed with script name
    as argument.  If argument is missing, it will ask to type the script
    name or simply return to regenerate more generic scripts located in
    runscr directory.  The scrips in runscr directory is called by the
    run script you are going to run.

   3.1  Available run scripts

      gsm ... Integrates global model for 72 hours.  The initial condition
              is a taylor made one stored in libs/con directory.

      rsm ... Integrates regional model for 48 hours.  The initial condition
              and boundary conditions are taylor made ones stored in libs/con
              directory.

      amip ... Perform amip run (long integration forced by observed SST).
               It has various options to compute monthly averages and
               do archive on different machines.

      cases ... Perform ensemble forecasts (many relatively long integrations).
                Various options are available for SST, SNOW, ICE and 
                archive methods.

      rsim ...  Perform rsm long integrations forced by global analysis (or
                forecasts).  Various options available for monthly average
                calculations and archives.

      rcases ... Perform many short rsm integrations forced by short global
                 forecasts.

      csim ... Perform rsm long integrations with larger domain regional 
               model forecast as boundary conditions (double nesting).

      gdas ... Perform global data assimilation.
